THE STUDENT DOES NOT LOSE THE DIFFERENCE, IT LEARNS A DIFFERENT ONE
2026-08-22, ten contrasts, so400m-full-a05

Issue #23 asked for one number the ten-set sweep had never recorded: how much
of the teacher's class axis the student inherits. It turned out the sweep had
been computing it ten times a run and throwing it away — probe_bisect.py
printed `class axis, student fp32 against pca 512` and never wrote it to
--json. One line of code, no new measurement, and the ten sets re-scored in
four minutes.

WHAT THE NUMBER IS

  d_t = mean(teacher, pos) - mean(teacher, neg)      in pca-512 space
  d_s = mean(student, pos) - mean(student, neg)      in the same space
  axis = cos(d_s, d_t)

The student is trained to emit the projected teacher vector, so the two live
in one 512-d space and the cosine is meaningful without any alignment step.
A difference of means also cancels anything shared by every frame, which is
why this is reported and the per-frame cos(student, teacher) is not — that
one measures the cone, reads 0.475 on stills and 0.428 on runs the board
scored 100% on, and predicts nothing. See probe_bisect.py:642.

THE COLUMN

  set             axis  teacher    pca  student    drop
  refrigerator   0.553    0.786  0.797    0.807  +0.010
  bowl           0.460    0.819  0.829    0.747  -0.082
  umbrella       0.408    0.715  0.725    0.478  -0.246
  toilet         0.405    0.579  0.582    0.448  -0.134
  laptop         0.352    0.781  0.767    0.679  -0.089
  bed            0.341    0.847  0.851    0.686  -0.165
  oven           0.305    0.850  0.870    0.612  -0.258
  glass          0.263    0.933  0.934    0.661  -0.274
  suitcase       0.200    0.894  0.894    0.687  -0.207
  book           0.141    0.907  0.910    0.648  -0.262
  mean           0.343    0.811  0.816    0.645  -0.171

`sep` is the pooled cross-scene AUC, the product metric. `drop` is student
minus pca — what distillation costs on that contrast.

THE FIRST FACT: THE CEILING IS 0.553 AND NOTHING IS NEAR 1.0

Not one of the ten contrasts has the student pointing where the teacher
points. The best is refrigerator at 0.553 and the mean is 0.343, which is a
57-degree error on a difference the student was trained to reproduce. This is
not a tail of hard cases; it is every row.

That rules out the reading the sep column invites. A student that had the
right axis and merely a noisier one would show a high cosine and a low AUC.
None of the ten does. The 0.171 mean drop is not the teacher's difference,
blurred — it is a different difference.

THE SECOND FACT: THE PROJECTION IS NOT WHERE IT GOES

pca minus teacher, over the ten sets: mean +0.005, range -0.013 to +0.019.
The frozen 1152->512 basis costs nothing measurable on any contrast here, so
the axis loss cannot be blamed on the 512-d bottleneck. It happens in the
distillation, between pca 512 and the student that is trained to hit it.

THE THIRD FACT: THE COLUMN TRACKS THE DROP, AND WEAKLY ENOUGH TO SAY SO

  r(axis, drop)                 = +0.775   perm p = 0.0085  (n=200k, two-sided)
  R^2                           =  0.600
  r(axis, pca)                  = -0.564   <- the confound
  r(pca,  drop)                 = -0.399
  partial r(axis, drop | pca)   = +0.726   perm p = 0.0180

The confound is a ceiling: an easy contrast has more room to fall, so a high
pca sep predicts a large drop by arithmetic alone. Controlling for it leaves
+0.726, so the axis is not just restating the ceiling.

It is still ten points. Swap the oven row for the hand-picked regeneration
of the same contrast (see 20260822-synth-oven-picked/) and the same figures
read +0.650 (p = 0.043) raw and +0.532 (p = 0.113) partial. One row moves
the partial correlation across the line. Quote the direction and the
mechanism; do not quote the coefficient as though it were measured.

  no constant is fitted here and none should be. The linear fit crosses zero
  drop at axis 0.626, above every row observed, which is an extrapolation
  from ten points and not a threshold anyone can use.

WHAT THIS NUMBER IS NOT

**It is not a cheap predictor.** It needs the labelled set, a teacher pass
and a student pass — exactly what computing sep needs. It costs nothing extra
once sep is being computed and it tells you nothing about a contrast you have
not measured. It does not join fit_check.py and it does not go near
enrolment. Every enrolment-time number tried so far has failed to predict a
run, and this one was never a candidate.

**It is not a per-frame agreement.** See above; that number exists, is 0.475,
and is meaningless.

**book is the extreme and nobody should read it yet.** Lowest inheritance of
the ten at 0.141 on a contrast the teacher scores 0.907 on, and separately
the only one of the ten that got *worse* when --infonce went 0.0 -> 0.3
(+0.043, against nine negatives; see 20260822-infonce-10contrast.log). Two
extremes on one row out of ten is a coincidence until a third set says
otherwise.

REPRODUCING

  for s in book glass laptop refrigerator oven toilet umbrella suitcase bowl bed; do
    d=20260822-synth-$s-crop; q=bench/stills/$d/queries.txt
    uv run --script tools/probe_bisect.py \
       --a bench/stills/$d/pos --b bench/stills/$d/neg \
       --pos "$(sed -n 1p $q)" --neg "$(sed -n 2p $q)" \
       --paired --keep bench/stills/$d/keep.txt --runs so400m-full-a05 \
       --json model/runs/_axis_a05_$s.json
  done

model/runs/ is gitignored; the JSONs above are the only place these numbers
live and they are reproducible from the checked-in stills in minutes.
